VT_TALK("sql/sql-dialects", {
 "standard": "SQL is an international standard, numbered ISO nine zero seven five, first published in nineteen eighty six. Every engine follows its core and then adds its own extras and spellings, which are called dialects. Each new version of the standard added features you have met in this course. Nineteen ninety two brought the JOIN keyword and CASE, nineteen ninety nine brought recursive queries and triggers, two thousand three brought window functions and MERGE, and twenty sixteen brought JSON. The query on this page uses only standard pieces and runs unchanged in all five big engines. Think of English spoken in different countries, with the same grammar and a few different words.",
 "diffs": "The same small task is often spelled differently. SQLite and PostgreSQL quote names with double quotes, MySQL uses backticks, and SQL Server uses square brackets. LIMIT gives the first few rows in SQLite, PostgreSQL and MySQL, while SQL Server writes TOP and the standard says FETCH FIRST. Joining text uses two bars in most engines, but CONCAT in MySQL and a plus sign in SQL Server. In the animation, pick a task and see five spellings side by side, then the SQLite one runs for real. One SQLite quirk stands out, LIKE ignores letter case for plain English letters, while equals does not. Think of the word for lift in British English and elevator in American English.",
 "choose": "There is no best database, only the best fit for a job. SQLite is a single file with no server, perfect inside phones, browsers and desktop apps. PostgreSQL is rich in features and extensions, and strict about the standard. MySQL and MariaDB are simple to run and supported by almost every web host. SQL Server fits companies built on Microsoft tools, Oracle serves very large enterprises, and DuckDB runs fast analytics on files right inside a notebook. Choose by asking how many users there are, how much data, which features you need, and what your team can run and back up safely. Real systems often use several engines at once. Think of choosing a bicycle, a car or a lorry depending on the load.",
 "portable": "If your SQL may run on more than one engine, a few habits help. Use COALESCE instead of local names such as IFNULL or NVL, CASE instead of IIF, and CAST instead of hidden conversions. Write joins with JOIN and ON, keep dates in the ISO year month day form, and list every non aggregated column in GROUP BY, because SQLite and older MySQL let you skip them while PostgreSQL refuses. Watch division too, ten divided by four gives two in most engines but two point five in MySQL. Think of writing a letter in plain words so readers from any region understand it.",
 "levels": "Here is the last ladder of this step. For a beginner, SQL is one language with local accents. At college level, know the standard versions and the common differences, and test on the engine you will really use. At masters level, tools such as SQLGlot translate queries between dialects. At research level, people prove when two queries always give the same rows, and build systems that turn plain questions into SQL. SQL began as SEQUEL at IBM in nineteen seventy four, and it has lasted because it says what you want and lets each engine find the fastest way. Think of a recipe that any kitchen can cook."
});
